<spell-notebook "markdown usage">
	<header level="1">
		Markdown
		<header>Markdown renders GitHub-flavoured markdown -- tables, task lists, code blocks -- from the element's
              own text or a file on this site.</>
	</>
	<markdown-tab-bar showing="usage"/>
	<section>
		<html>
			<ui-menu class="ui-docs-section-tabs" appearance="segmented" aria-label="Sections">
				<ui-item link="#using-it">Using it</>
				<ui-item link="#rendering">Rendering</>
				<ui-item link="#events">Events</>
				<ui-item link="#keyboard">Keyboard</>
				<ui-item link="#accessibility">Accessibility</>
			</>
		</>
	</>

	<!-- USAGE:  from the old Astro page's Behaviour, plus the vocabulary's events and the shadow markup's a11y. -->
	<section label="Using it">
		<markdown>
			Write markdown inside `<ui-markdown>`, or point `source` at a file of this site. The text comes from, first match
			wins:

			- `source`: a file of this site (same origin only), fetched when `load` says (`eager`, `visible` or `idle`). Its
			  relative links and images resolve beside it, so a README's links work where it's shown.
			- A `<script type="text/markdown">` child: its text exactly, so `<tags>` in code stay text.
			- The element's own text, dedented.

			The `content` property replaces either until `source` changes. Rendering (marked) loads lazily, with the first
			markdown shown;  sanitizing (DOMPurify) only with `sanitized`.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Rendering">
		<markdown>
			- GitHub-flavoured: tables, task lists, strikethrough, autolinks.
			- Fenced code blocks become `<ui-code language="x" copy>`: one highlighter and one palette for the whole page.
			- Raw HTML is kept as written unless `sanitized`, which runs it through DOMPurify (its own lazy chunk):
			  set it for text you didn't write.
			- Headings get GitHub's ids, so `#anchor` links work, inside the element too (the page's own fragment
			  navigation can't see into its shadow root). `heading-offset` shifts their levels to sit under the page's
			  heading (property `headingLevelOffset`: `headingOffset` is WebKit's native `headingoffset`).
			- `headings` lists the headings (`{ level, text, id }`), e.g. for a table of contents; `ui-drawn` fires with
			  them after each render.
			- Scrolling to a heading from outside: `reveal(id)` scrolls to it and puts `#id` in the address. A plain
			  `<a href="#id">` elsewhere on the page works too: the element follows `hashchange`, and a page opened
			  with `#id` in its address lands there after the first render. `--ui-markdown-heading-scroll-margin`
			  leaves room for a sticky header.
			- `skip-title` drops the text's leading `#` title (the page shows its own headline above a README); the
			  text itself keeps it.
			- `editable`: Write / Preview tabs. The preview draws as the element does, swapping only the blocks an
			  edit changed. Each keystroke is a `ui-changed` and makes the element dirty; `save()` writes the text back
			  to `source`.
			- Spell's own markdown engine, which draws with `ui-*` elements (headers, lists, tables, messages for
			  alerts, checkboxes for tasks), is the app's `<spell-markdown>`: a subclass, outside Spell UI.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Events">
		<markdown>
			- `ui-drawn` -- rendered: `{ headings }`.
			- `ui-loaded` -- the text arrived, from `source` or the element's own content: `{ source, content }`.
			- `ui-error` (cancelable) -- loading or rendering failed: `{ kind, source, error }`. Cancel it to skip the error
			  message.
			- `ui-changed`, `ui-saving`, `ui-saved` -- editing and saving, shared with `<ui-code>` and `<ui-include>` (see
			  Include's Usage tab).
		</>
		<markdown>
			```js
			const readme = document.querySelector("ui-markdown")
			readme.addEventListener("ui-drawn", (event) => {
			  for (const { level, text, id } of event.detail.headings) console.log(level, text, `#${id}`)
			})
			```
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Keyboard">
		<markdown>
			Nothing of its own: links, task-list checkboxes and code blocks inside are ordinary tab stops. `editable`'s
			tabs are one tab stop: the arrow keys move between Write and Preview, Home and End to either end.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Accessibility">
		<markdown>
			- The markdown renders into a real `<article>` with real headings, lists and tables, so it reads like the page
			  around it. Use `heading-offset` to keep the page outline in order.
			- Task-list checkboxes are named by their item's text (marked leaves them unlabelled).
			- Code blocks are `<ui-code>`, each named by its language.
			- While `source` loads, a `<ui-loader>` (`role="status"`) says so; a failed load shows a `<ui-message>` saying
			  why.
		</>
	</>
</spell-notebook>
